In article <3sv0q5$mon@www.uno.edu>, emurphy@www.uno.edu (Edward Murphy) writes:
>What is the cause of the 8 character limit on passwords?
> 
>Recap: I've just installed wu-ftpd 2.4 on our DEC Alpha OSF/1 3.2
>system with  C2 security enhancements. The max password length is 10.
>Users who have passwords of length greater than 8 cannot use the WU
>ftpd programs, they get "Login incorrect".

[other detail deleted]

The problem lies with the password encryption call, crypt().
It only hashes the first eight characters of the supplied password,
producing the familiar 13-character string that is seen in /etc/passwd.

For C2 security, when longer passwords are permitted (as in your case),
the encryption routine must be repeatedly called with successive
segments of the password to produce a longer encrypted string.
DON'T PANIC: all of this is achieved using the (undocumented) call
bigcrypt(). Use it in exactly the same way as crypt() is used and
your "long" passwords will be checked correctly.

Richard
