In article <don.47.00CE0E50@tripath.colosys.net>,
don@tripath.colosys.net (Don Wilkerson) writes:
don> Just interested in y'alls thoughts.  When setting up users I know
don> you can choose which shell to use.  I was wondering what do you
don> think would be the better shell /bin/csh or /bin/sh and why?

Definitely not /bin/sh -- it just doesn't have the features users
expect in an interactive shell: no history, no command line editing,
no job control.

If you are limiting yourself to software provided with the system, the
real choice is between /bin/csh and /bin/ksh.  My sympathies are with
/bin/ksh -- it provides just about all the functionality csh does, it
has the cleaner Bourne shell command language (csh control constructs
are much uglier), and it is very close to the POSIX 1003.2 standard
shell, so something reasonably similar should exist on any 1003.2
compliant system.

Around here, the actual choice is tcsh or bash, where tcsh is a more
featureful csh and bash is a more featureful ksh, both freely
distributable.  Our default is tcsh, partly due to inertia and partly
because most of the introductory documentation around here (esp. "Unix
for VMS users", which used to be an important doc for us) is csh
oriented.  Among experienced users, it is probably about 50/50
tcsh/bash.
