In Article <don.47.00CE0E50@tripath.colosys.net>
don@tripath.colosys.net (Don Wilkerson) writes:
>Just interested in y'alls thoughts.  When setting up users I know you can 
>choose which shell to use.  I was wondering what do you think would be the 
>better shell /bin/csh or /bin/sh and why?

sh is the most basic of the shells, but has very few features and is not very
user friendly, IMHO. I use it to write scripts, but that's about it. csh is
much nicer, but still lacks many useful features. I would suggest tcsh. It is a
superset of csh so the users .cshrc and .login files still work, but it has a
huge number of added features including on-screen command line editing, command
and filename completion, idle terminal logout, and much more flexible prompts.

I hold the belief that the only reason all users don't run tcsh is that they
are not aware of its existence.

