In <DSR.95Jun27150145@lns100.lns.cornell.edu> dsr@lns598.lns.cornell.edu (Daniel S. Riley) writes:


>In article <danpop.803827853@rscernix> Dan.Pop@mail.cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
>Dan.Pop> If the developer cannot be sure that he managed to do this
>Dan.Pop> before shipping, he will replace assert() with proper error
>Dan.Pop> detection and reporting code.  Joe User should _never_ see
>Dan.Pop> something looking like "assertion failed...".
>
>So DEC should redefine assert to print out a polite error message?  I
>fail to see how that does any good--it is still an incomprehensible
>error message.  Whether it says "assertion failed" followed by some
>gibberish or "A fatal error has occurred, please submit an SPR to DEC
>with this information" followed by some gibberish makes little
>difference to me.  Replacing every "assert()" with a more informative
>message would be a waste of engineering time, and a undesirable
>impediment to free use of assert().

Even if it makes little difference to you, it makes a lot of difference
to the confused user who doesn't understand what's wrong with his Fortran
code that it doesn't link properly.  A C programmer, at least knows what
an assert is and understands immediately that the fault isn't in his
files.  The C illiterate programmer isn't (and shouldn't be) required to
know what a C assert is in order to compile and link his programs.

Internal consistency checks are a good thing, using assert() to report
their failure isn't.  Those who invented NDEBUG knew what they were
doing.

If a program detects itself that it's broken, it can let the user know
the bad news in a friendly, non confusing, manner and assert() isn't
the right tool for this.

Dan
