Richard Stallman inquired about why M-SPC on DEC OSF/1 introduces the
compose character sequence.

As far as I can tell it has to do with the way the X libraries (X11R5
vintage) are set up on DEC OSF/1, and how they do XLookupString
processing.  M-SPC also turns on composition mode inside xterm.  I do
not know of a way to turn this off with xmodmap or X resources.

My xmodmap output is:

xmodmap:  up to 3 keys per modifier, (keycodes in parentheses):

shift       Shift_L (0xae),  Shift_R (0xab)
lock        Caps_Lock (0x74)
control     Control_L (0xaf),  Control_R (0xb0)
mod1        Meta_L (0xac),  Meta_L (0xb1),  Alt_R (0xb2)
mod2        Multi_key (0xad)
mod3      
mod4      
mod5        Alt_R (0xb2)

Both Alt_R and Meta_L induce this behavior from the spacebar; if I
remove Alt_R from modifier 1, it no longer induces this behavior.
If I convert some other key (say, what's normally KP_0) to Alt_L, and
add it to modifier 1, then it too induces the spacebar composition.

There is also a separate keysym (Multi_key) which I use on a different
key on my keyboard, and it acts just fine--like a normal "dead key" that
introduces a compose sequence.

I think this is just something that DEC wants its X libraries to
support--I recall that you could do composition on its VT220 keyboards
in a similar way by hitting Compose-Space and then the two characters to
be composed.

So, my questions are:
* is this desired behavior from the DEC X11 libraries?
* is there a way to disable it?

#ifdef OSF1
		  /* OSF/1 XLookupString is also too eager with
                     certain meta/alt bits, particularly for thinks like
		     Meta-SPC which will start compose character processing
		     if you don't mask out the meta stuff. */
		  event.xkey.state &= ~(ControlMask | 
					dpyinfo->meta_mod_mask | 
					dpyinfo->alt_mod_mask | 
					dpyinfo->super_mod_mask | 
					dpyinfo->hyper_mod_mask);
#else
		  event.xkey.state &= ~ControlMask;
#endif
		  nbytes = XLookupString (&event.xkey, copy_buffer,
					  80, &keysym, &compose_status);

