newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <3s75k4$3v@irc.ucsd.edu>, csieler@waynesworld.ucsd.edu (Chris
Sieler) wrote:

> Hello,
> 
>         We have recently aquired an AlphaStation 200 4/166 that will be a 
> UNIX host for our company.  Aside from getting used to a System V style
UNIX, things seem to be going well.  I can't seem to figure out how to get
my HP 4mm DATdrive to work with my system.  I've investigated the man
pages for sysconfig, and for dump, but I can really find no help on
loading device drivers.  Can anyonepoint be to a good jumping off point on
getting this done.  I've got the Nemeth
> et al Sys Admin book, but its reference to OSF/1 in this area are
lacking.  Any
> and all help is appreciated.

    Chris -

    There are at least two things you need to do:

1. get the HP poop sheet on how to configure the DIP switches on the
bottom of the drive (different settings for DEC machines than for some
others), AND

2. get the OSF/1 system administration guide (the part number under OSF/1
2.0 was AA-PS2RB-TE), which discusses how to add 3rd-party disk and tape
devices. Unfortunately, you may have to add/modify information in
cam_data.c to use all the capability of the drive (e.g. firmware
compression) if doconfig doesn't recognize the drive as "media
cpmp[ation"-capable device (e.g., the TLZ07).

    In general, though, after powering down the system, attaching the
drive, and rebooting the generic kernel (/genvmunix), you need to rebuild
the kernel with doconfig in single-user mode, and see if the device
special files it builds work for you (you should get a complete set of
low, regular, and high-density devices --- /dev/rmt0l, /dev/rmt0m, and
/dev/rmt0h, as well as /dev/nrmt0{l, m, h} for non-rewinding). Otherwise,
modify the SCSI CAM file and do a MAKEDEV on tznn (where nn is the SCSI
lun) for device type tzxx (or whatever your CAM entry is).

     It really is worth buying the manuals.

                  Joe Gurman

