In <3s9j2b$i66@news.duke.edu>,
gallatin@dali.isds.duke.edu (Andrew Gallatin) wrote:
gallatin> As far as I know, sysconfig has only worked since 3.0.
gallatin> Maybe AFS is/was written for an older version of the OS and
gallatin> Transarc didn't figure it was worth their time to make it
gallatin> loadable.

In article <danpop.803757733@rscernix>,
Dan.Pop@mail.cern.ch (Dan Pop) responded:
Dan.Pop> Nope.  Being part of the kernel, AFS has to be updated
Dan.Pop> anytime DEC releases a new version of OSF (i.e. 2 or 3 times
Dan.Pop> per year :-)

That they release a new version does not invalidate Andrew's point at
all.  They (obviously) update previous versions, not rewrite from
scratch, so they are working from a version set up to be linked into
the kernel on Alphas.  A major market pressure on Transarc these days
(e.g. from Hepix) is to be get new OS version support out more
quickly, so they're going to do as little as possible to get working
versions out for 3.0 and 3.2.  If adapting AFS to be a loadable kernel
module takes extra work, Transarc won't do it until they get caught up
enough that Hepix (and their other customers) stop complaining about
the lag in current OS version support.  The argument that Digital UNIX
must not support loadable kernel modules because AFS doesn't use them
is completely bogus.

