In <3sbh8o$1ug@kani.Helsinki.FI> havia@cc.helsinki.fi (Jyrki Havia) writes:

>Dan.Pop@mail.cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
>
>> ld crashing with an "assertion failed..." message shows that DEC ships
>> test versions of the vital utilities as production quality software.
>> There is no excuse for leaving active assert's in production software.
>
>	Yes, it is MUCH MORE productive and customer friendly to let ld to
>	produce incorrect code, than to include annoying debugging code.
>
>	Indeed, I see.

Nope, you don't.  You use proper error handling code in production 
versions, which will give a meaningful error message to the user and
enough debugging information to the developer.

assert() is, by definition, for those problems that are fixed before the
product is shipped.  If the developer cannot be sure that he managed to
do this before shipping, he will replace assert() with proper error
detection and reporting code.  Joe User should _never_ see something
looking like "assertion failed...".  This is a minimal form of respect
towards the paying customer: if you can't give (err, sell) him bug-free
software, at least don't give him programs that barf this kind of
gibberish when they fail.

Dan
