That's because News interaction is a purely voluntary and personal
function performed by members of the Usenet capable public, some of
whom happen to work for Digital. It is not, and never has been, an
official Digital bug reporting channel or an official Digital help line.

You may also notice that other vendors don't provide replies in
"their" newsgroups, either. You should read those disclaimers at the
bottom ("These are my opinions, and have nothing to do with company X
which I work for.")

Maybe your post didn't get through. Maybe some Digital people were
officially caring about a bug or enhancement that has been reported
through official channels, and were too busy to read News. Maybe they
were on customer sites and didn't read News while your post was there.
Maybe they were sick or on holiday. There has never been a guarantee
in *any* newsgroup that anything you post will be fully and correctly
responded to, or even that it will be responded to at all. This forum
is *free*. (I'd be surprised if a user at a .edu site was paying for
it.) You get what you pay for.

> We have another SUN Sparc 5.  It had problems with BIND & NIS.
> When I was asked for help to solve the problem, I quickly found
> an FAQ entry in Solaris's FAQ.

Maybe this is a big problem with SUN, therefore it's in the FAQ. Your
problems may have been specialised, in which case they shouldn't be
in the FAQ (which does, after all, stand for *Frequently* Asked
Questions.)

