In Article <3sdvrn$2sc@cismsun.univ-lyon1.fr>
Jean MAUPAS <jm@upcl.univ-lyon1.fr> writes:
>Hi,
>we have experienced the following problem, using X applications on a DEC
>3000/300 running OSF V3.2:
>
>X Toolkit Warning: Cannot allocate colormap entry for "tan1"
>Cannot allocate colormap entry for "#e5e5e5"
>Cannot allocate colormap entry for default background
>
>and some colors are missing on the screen...
>
>How to increase the size of this colormap table ???

This table is tied hardware and is (for most graphics boards) exactly 256
entries long. Unless you choose to install a private color map for an
application, all applications running at any time share the 256 slots in the
color table. Some, like Netscape and Mosaic, tend to grab most everything
available. I always run Netscape with the -install qualifier so it uses a
separate color space and does not interfere with everything else.

The down-side of installing a private colormap for an app is that the colormap
is only loaded into the hardware colormap when the application is the active
one. The rest of the time the colors in my Netscape window look pretty much
random.

Any application displaying a full-color image is likely to use most, if not all
available slots in the color table and may not be able to allocate everything
it wants. Some tools, such as xv, go to great length to make things look fairly
good by "borrowing" colors used by other applications or using similar colors
when needed. But, once you have loaded an image, odds are that the colormap is
completely full and any application that tries to start up while the image is
being displayed will be unable to allocate colors.

R. Kevin Oberman			
Energy Sciences Network (ESnet)
National Energy Research Supercomputer Center (NERSC)
Lawrence Livermore National Laboratory (LLNL)
