In article <SCOTT.95Jun22090014@death.dmi.stevens-tech.edu>, Scott J. Kolodzieski (scott@death.dmi.stevens-tech.edu) wrote:

 > >To me, DEC OSF/1 looks like a memory hungry OS (the worst in this respect
 > >I have ever used) without any outstanding merits over its competitors.
 > >

 > I agree, we run Pro/Engineer quite a bit where I work, on all supported 
 > unix platforms minimum memory/swap is ~ 32MB/100MB except for OSF/1 where 
 > the minimum is 64MB/300MB. 

Well, yes -- Two of the design criteria for DEC OSF/1 would seem to have
been:

  1.  Memory is cheap.
  2.  Disk is cheaper.

... which explains why OSF/1 doesn't mind using gobloads of RAM, and,
if it can get it, orders of magnitude more disk space.

To me, that doesn't actually count as a point against it.  When you
compare the bang-for-buck you get out of MIPS, SPARC and POWER, I'd
take Alpha with OSF/1 any day of the week.

The application base is still a bit problematic, though...

