check his environment carefully; he might discover some simple fix that
would alleviate the problem.  For example, if your swap space is too low, 
increasing it can have a dramatic effect on performance.  If your file
server is slow, then even the fastest machine can be bogged down.  For a
while, my machine was really slow because we had problems with the Ethernet
segment.  Once a failing piece of network hardware was replaced, my machine 
started performing at its usual impressive speed.

   Our customers are very smart.  However, it's all too easy to overlook 
the real source of the problem.  

As for your system performance problem and complaint about the kernel
size, I can say two things:

1. Work is underway to make the operating system leaner and meaner.  

2. There are things you can do as well, such as making sure you have 
enough swap space.  There are a lot of details in the release notes and 
related system documentation. 

|>PS: Why is DEC not supporting the Linux port?
|>    (Or is it to good?)

Well, given the enthusiastic response and attendance to Linus Torvald's 
recent lecture here, it's my understanding that the Linux porting efforts 
are going quite well.  As for support, it's my understanding he was given
Alpha hardware to do a port.  
