In article <danpop.803827853@rscernix> Dan.Pop@mail.cern.ch (Dan Pop) writes:
Dan.Pop> assert() is, by definition, for those problems that are fixed
Dan.Pop> before the product is shipped.

An assertion is not "for" a particular problem at all, it is an
assertion that a particular condition is true.  The proper use is to
assert that a program invariant has not been violated.  assert()
should be the early warning system for unknown bugs, not a test for a
particular problem.

For development tools like ld, I would *much* rather have assertions
enabled--if ld has a bug, I'd rather get an immediate indication than
have to track down an obscure corruption in the resulting executable.

Dan.Pop> If the developer cannot be sure that he managed to do this
Dan.Pop> before shipping, he will replace assert() with proper error
Dan.Pop> detection and reporting code.  Joe User should _never_ see
Dan.Pop> something looking like "assertion failed...".

So DEC should redefine assert to print out a polite error message?  I
fail to see how that does any good--it is still an incomprehensible
error message.  Whether it says "assertion failed" followed by some
gibberish or "A fatal error has occurred, please submit an SPR to DEC
with this information" followed by some gibberish makes little
difference to me.  Replacing every "assert()" with a more informative
message would be a waste of engineering time, and a undesirable
impediment to free use of assert().

