For about a year now, I have been closely monitoring both this newsgroup
and the AlphaOsfManagers mailing list, watching for problems relating to
DECladebug, the product which I work on.  There are a few bugs reported
or questions asked each week, but by and large, the volume is quite low.
In an effort to improve the product, I always try to post an answer or
send mail to the individual with the problem.  I'm often quite surprised
to find that while people are willing to complain about products (not
just DECladebug or Digital products in general, either), they are not 
willing to report the problems through the necessary channels to get them
fixed.  If problems go unreported, they are very difficult to fix.  And
more often than not, the problem is overly vague.

While the internet is an immensely valuable tool, it is not the "official"
way to report bugs.  However, it is often much cheaper and more practical
for Digital and the customers to use this medium for that purpose.  If all
of the energy that the software development community spent on bashing 
each other went into efficiently reporting problems, we would all be a lot
further along and a lot happier.

I speak for myself, as a software developer; not as a representative
of Digital Equipment Corporation.

