I don't know why somebody would say that.  I do know why I would say
just the opposite though.  Digital UNIX (nee DEC OSF/1) is *ahead* of
other unixes as far as I can see in the following areas:

o 64 bits.  As far as I know, Digital UNIX was the first 64bit unix to
run on a desktop/deskside machine.  They've already been through the
pains of migrating, SGI and Sun are just starting..

o NFS v3.  Digital developed NFS v3 with Sun.  They were the first to
have it deployed (in OSF/1 3.0, August 1994).  SGI did follow soon (6
mo?) after; but from all reports I've seen, their's was a very buggy
product.   Sun has yet to kick v3 out the door with Solaris 2.5.

o Journaling File Systems.  AdvFS has always been stable on our
machines, and has saved us from hours of fsck'ing after power outages.
If you buy the tools, you can even do neat things like re-partition a
machine while its running, stripe file, etc.

o Human manageability.  I've managed HP-UX systems, and I find their
start up scripts to be quite a mess becuase they were designed to be
manipulated with GUI tools that I don't trust.  I'd imagine that AIX
is nearly as bad.  Under Digital UNIX, I've found that the only file
that I need to read & edit in many cases is /etc/rc.config, which sets
environment variables used later in the init process.  This is much
cleaner than Solaris or SunOS.

o X.  Digital ships a usable X11 setup out of the box.  Pretty much
straight MIT X11R5 (R6 is available in preview form as well).  This is
one of the few OS'es where you can build and install X/Motif apps w/o
having to go out and build the MIT distribution.  Plus, they use an
almost totally standard xdm setup for console logins.  CDE is even
shipping with 3.2, should you want it.

I think that Digital UNIX is the *best* commercial unix out there,
miles ahead of offerings from Sun, HP, SGI and IBM.  My only complaint
is that it's a tad bit bloated, and I've found that a minimum of
48M/workstation is required to do any real work.  This is even
supposed to be fixed in 4.0

Drew

