OSF/1 may be dead or whatever, Digital UNIX (aka DEC OSF/1) certainly is
alive and well. Technically DEC OSF/1 and Digital UNIX are the same. The
namechange reflects the fact that X/Open now licenses the right to use 
the name UNIX and DEC OSF/1 happens to fullfill all the requirements
to get this license. With X/Open now defining when something entitles to
get the license to carry the name UNIX, all the discussions about 'who 
has the real unix and when is unix unix' have come to an end. 

re: Mark's quote 'OSF/1 is a lot more SVR4 than BSD'. He probably refers
to Digital UNIX (aka DEC OSF/1) in this case and is certainly right. 
Since quite some time Digital UNIX conforms with both SVID2 and SVID3
unlike some of the other popular u*x systems that are not a direct 
implementation of SVR4.
By installing the optional (royality bearing) 'System V Extensions 
for Digital UNIX' you get a system that looks and acts like SVR4 
(including things like sysadm, sar, truss and lots of other stuff). 

