Mark Saltzman (marks@wing.net) wrote:
: I'm not crazy about learning yet another unix when the world seems to 
: be standardizing on SVR4.  

I don't think it's fair to say that "the world seems to be standardizing
on SVR4."  Certain vendors have chosen to base their operating systems 
largely on SVR4, others have chosen the OSF/1 model (not necessarily
DEC's implementation of it).  

If there's been one constant about the slice of the world that U*ix
occupies, it's that there is no "standard."  We seem to be converging
quickly towards something that seems very much like a standard, but I
think we're still quite some distance from it.

OSF/1 is a lot more SVR4 than BSD.  And as it continues to evolve, it
will seem more so.  POSIX and X/Open are quite SVR4-ish, while taking
advantage of some of the best aspects of BSD.  OSF/1 complies with
POSIX and X/Open (whatever that means on this particular day), so you
wouldn't be "learning yet another Unix," so much as adapting what you
already know to the almost-standards towards which we're all heading.

I would venture a guess that you'd be quite happy with DEC OSF/1 (or
whatever we're calling it these days) on an Alpha.  I administer a 
fairly large number of various Alpha systems, and I've been quite
impressed with the reliability and performance of the hardware and 
operating system (as of version 3.0).  Porting has not been an issue
since the free software folks have had a chance to adapt their code
(adaptations which are in most cases quite minor).

