> o Journaling File Systems.  AdvFS has always been stable on our
> machines, and has saved us from hours of fsck'ing after power outages.
> If you buy the tools, you can even do neat things like re-partition a
> machine while its running, stripe file, etc.

I really wish this were true for me. I've lost more hours in cleaning
up from AdvFS disasters than I could ever save in fsck'ing. It's clear
to me that AdvFS was an immature technology, rushed out the door without
the infrastructure to support it for real work. In particular:

	- the restore procedure does not restore quotas. The there is
	  no way to backup and restore a logically equivalent volume
	  without the intervention of scripts or humans. This is 
	  unacceptable for a production file system
	- no network dump/restore, at least in 3.0
	- the AdvFS file system recovery tools are undocumented and,
	  in our case, crashed our system every time we used them
	- AdvFS is over-sensitive to hardware failures. Bad disk block
	  in the metadata? Panic the system...
	- No performance tools. While there is an undocumented advfsstats
	  command, there's  no explanation as to what it means, and the
	  the Polycenter Performance layered products just ignore AdvFS
	  to the extent that you can't even determine buffer effectiveness
	- You can't tell how much file space is left, especially on
	  filesets with lots of little files. The utilities give you
	  contradictory percent-frees, and you have the issues of
	  running out of metadata space. The 3.0 release workaround
	  (touch many, many files) doesn't work reliably. Who knows
	  about 3.2.

Oh, and if this weren't enough, if you run All-AdvFS, you can't even
turn off the UFS buffering. If you set it to 0%, it goes to 10%. At best
you still have 1% of your memory devoted to unused UFS buffering. Not
a big deal in and of itself, but a clear sign of how poorly this
is integrated into the overall OS.

AdvFS holds great promise, promise that is unrealized for me. I truly
regret the day I said: "Think of all the fsck time I'll be saving..."

	- Saul
