This is not going to help you, but:

I have seen the same problem on one of our systems (a 3000/400,
64MB memory, 128 MB swap) that we upgraded from V2.0 to V3.2.
The problem is the linker, and it seems to be time related:
shorter jobs compile/link no problem, but longer jobs 
(i.e. > 20 sec cpu time for ld) freeze the system. Yet the same 
job causes no problems on an identical V2.1 system.

I played with the "ubc-borrowpercent" parameter, with mixed results.
Setting setting it to "20" didn't make a difference; a 
"ubc-borrowpercent = 80" finally delayed the onset of the disk thrashing, 
but didn't stop it: once ld racks up a certain number of cpu cycles 
the system becomes useless.

My users are frustrated and/or mad as hell and have even asked me to 
downgrade to V2. Unfortunately that's not an option, and neither is
adding more memory (which doesn't seem to help anyways). I am ready
to file an SPR because this behavior is totally unacceptable.


