Andrew Gallatin (gallatin@hpdemo.isds.duke.edu) wrote:

: o Journaling File Systems.  AdvFS has always been stable on our
: machines, and has saved us from hours of fsck'ing after power outages.
: If you buy the tools, you can even do neat things like re-partition a
: machine while its running, stripe file, etc.

Word of warning here: *Don't* run an AdvFS partition for your news spool.
We tried it (in order to use two disks as one partition), and had a number
of headaches when the disk refused to be written to ("disk full") when there
was plenty of free space and free inodes.

Apparently, the AdvFS wants contiguous space for non-inode metadata, and 
creating and removing lots of small files (news articles) causes the disk to 
run out of contiguous space before the disk is really full.

DEC's solution?  Immeadiatly after you format the disk create and remove
hundreds of thousands of 0 byte files.  This will pre-allocate the metadata
space.  (We tried this, but still ran into the problem.)

Now /alt lives on it's own drive.

In fairness, several other partitions are AdvFS and are doing fine.

