In <3s9j2b$i66@news.duke.edu> gallatin@dali.isds.duke.edu (Andrew Gallatin) writes:

>In article <danpop.803736595@rscernix>, Dan Pop <Dan.Pop@mail.cern.ch> wrote:
>
>>I recently installed AFS (Andrew, not Advanced) on 3.2 and the installation
>>involved rebuilding the kernel.  If Transarc decided to do it this way
>>(they supply loadable modules for other OS's) maybe they know something.
>
>As far as I know, sysconfig has only worked since 3.0.  Maybe AFS
>is/was written for an older version of the OS and Transarc didn't
>figure it was worth their time to make it loadable.  Or perhaps

Nope.  Being part of the kernel, AFS has to be updated anytime DEC
releases a new version of OSF (i.e. 2 or 3 times per year :-)

There is a version for 3.0 and another for 3.2.

>>I would have expected the "*best* commercial unix out there, miles
>>ahead of offerings from Sun, HP, SGI and IBM" to have more features and
>>less problems than its "inferior" competitors.  This doesn't seem to be
>>the case, however.
>
>For me it does, for you it apparently does not.  It looks like our
>milage varies.  I place a lot of importance on NFS v3, advfs, the
>clean system startup files, and the lack of bugs that have bitten me
>personally (as opposed to HP-UX for example, which doesn't have NFS v3, 
>doesn't even let you partition a disk, has a twisty maze of nasty

If you mean older versions of HP-UX and the _boot_ disk, then you're
right.  Otherwise, not.

>startup files, and where I've been bitten by lots of bugs). 

Using AFS, I couldn't care less about NFSv3.  And if you have a
heterogeneous network (as we have: 7 different commercial flavours of
Unix), you can really use only those features available everywhere.

Others have posted their horror stories with AdvFS, so I won't insist.
The "inferior" AIX has had a pretty good journalled FS for years.  And
the current version of HP-UX comes (or will come) with a JFS too (and
it lets you partion the boot disk).

ld crashing with an "assertion failed..." message shows that DEC ships
test versions of the vital utilities as production quality software.
There is no excuse for leaving active assert's in production software.

To me, DEC OSF/1 looks like a memory hungry OS (the worst in this respect
I have ever used) without any outstanding merits over its competitors.
The days when 4 GB of address space won't be enough haven't arrived here,
so there's nothing to compensate the disadvantage of having tens of MB of
RAM "wasted" by the OS.

Dan
