organization: Wilson Lab, Cornell U., Ithaca, NY 14853
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1


In article <3rpviv$iaf@amhux3.amherst.edu>,
pdchapin@news.amherst.edu (PAUL D. CHAPIN) writes:

pdchapin> Is it my imagination or does DEC Unix 3.2 have a bug in the
pdchapin> find command.  In particular, the command "find
pdchapin> /usr/fac/pdchapin -name test1 -o -name test2 -print" only
pdchapin> finds the second file.

I believe that is the correct behavior -- and has precedence over or,
so your expression is equivalent to

find /usr/fac/pdchapin -name test1 -o \( -name test2 -print \)

The second term is never evaluated when the first is true, so -print
is never evaluated when "-name test1" is true.  To get the behavior
you want, do

find /usr/fac/pdchapin \( -name test1 -o -name test2 \) -print

