In article <3rvahl$jdk@badboy.iprolink.ch>, John McCluckie <mclucky@iprolink.ch> writes:
|>
|>Hi guys and gals,
|>I have just spent a few moments (ha ha ha) upgrading our DEC Alpha 
|>4000-710 from OSF1 V1.3 to V3.0 (via 2.0) and now i'm sunk.....
|>From 1.3 to 2.0 was a breeze.... I event went out to eat something
|>the system rebooted, all devices were there and i had a unlimited
|>number of logins.
|>Now from 2.0 to 3.0 all was as before except ! yes now in the LMF 
|>database I have this new entry called DEC-USR, (as well as the original
|>DEC-BASE), which i can't modify and which allows only root logins or 
|>nologins at all, i've tried deleting this and re-installing the old,
|>nope..,i've tried modifying ..nope. 
|>So any good ideas would be appreciated...like buy HP.....
|>
|>
|>				thanks in advance JOhn M
|>

Sounds like you have an OSF-BASE PAK and the automatically installed OSF-USR PAK
installed on your system.  The OSF-BASE PAK entitles you to two non-root users.
It is the OSF-USR PAK that was automatically installed which actually provides
the ability to have the two users.

If this is true, you should be able to have two users login.  If you do a
"lmf list" it should show a status of "active" for both OSF-USR and OSF-BASE.
If it doesn't, then there is something wrong with the PAK (it expired or was
entered incorrectly).

Dave



