From: gurman@uvsp.gsfc.nasa.gov (Joseph B. Gurman)
Subject: Re: HOW TO MAKE A BACKUP?
Date: 1995/06/13
Message-ID: <gurman-1306951434130001@barkochba.gsfc.nasa.gov>
X-Deja-AN: 104317385
references: <3rik4l$feu@news.ycc.yale.edu>
organization: NASA Goddard Space Flight Center -- Greenbelt, Maryland USA
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

In article <3rik4l$feu@news.ycc.yale.edu>, walid@minerva.cis.yale.edu
(Walid Majid) wrote:

> can someone please tell me how to make a backup of everything on an osf 
> machine.  at the moment the tapedrive is not even connected to the scsi 
> port.  i guess the first thing i need to do is to determine the device 
> number.  how would i do this?

    Walid -

    Despite this enormous opportunity for a Harvard grad to make sport of
Yalies, I will allow the chance to slip by.... (Cf. preterition in the
dictionary.)

    You need to:

1. Bring down your system (shutdown -h now), and power it off;

2. attach the SCSI tape drive to the SCSI daisy chain (e.g., the external
SCSI port if there are no external SCSI devices yet), MAKING CERTAIN THAT
THE SCSI LUN IS SET TO A VALUE DIFFERENT FROM THAT OF ANY DEVICE CURRENTLY
ON THE SAME SCSI BUS (by not telling us what model of OSF/1 machine, we're
unable to be more specific: some machines, such as the 3000/300 series,
have only a single, internal + external SCSI, while the other 3000 series
workstations have separate internal and external SCSI's, some servers can
have several, and so on);

3. power up the machine and interrupt it using the interrupt switch (on
the back of desktop workstations, on the front of the 3000/800 and /900)
to get the >>>
console prompt;

4. at the >>> prompt, enter 'show device' (or 'sh dev') to make certain
the system can "see" the SCSI tape; note that if you have multiple SCSI
buses, the
unit number may seem strange (e.g., lun 5 on the second bus will give you
DKB500 in the "sh dev" display [a la OpenVMS], but tz13 (since the first
SCSI bus = 8 devices, including the controller) under unix);

5. if the system can see the device, resume booting (usually, the "boot"
command will do);

6. log in as root;

7. copy /vmunix to someplace safe;

8. cp /genvmunix /vmunix;

9. reboot (you're now booting off the generic kernel): shutdown -r now;

10. log in as root again;

11. shut down to single-user mode: "shutdown now" (so no one plays around
with the system while you're rebuilding the kernel, which you do with);

12. /sbin/bcheckrc (to fsck and mount all local file systems, including /usr);

13. doconfig (it will ask you for the name of the system config file,
which will be of the form /sys/conf/YOURSYSTEMNAME, and whether you want
to create a new one, which you do, and whether you want to edit it, which
you don't --- unless using ed is your idea of fun; it will also ask which
of a variety of kernel options you want; unless your very short of memory
or / disk space, select them all);

14. wait 5 - 10 minutes for the kernel to be built;

15. note the file path for the new kernel (to be used in step 17);

16. boot again (left as an exercise for the student);

17. copy the new kernel to /vmunix; and

18. reboot (also left as an exercise for the student).

    Voila! You should be able to see the tape device (if OSF/1 can
understand it, that is....). You can check this by doing an "ls
/dev/*rzN*" where N is the SCSI lun (remember to add 8 to the lun on the
device if it's on a second SCSI bus).

    But wait: you wanted to back up all the file systems on the machine.
Sadly, you will have to use dump. If you are using a DAT or Exabyte drive,
and can back up several file systems to a single tape, you'll want to dump
to the file /dev/nrmt0h (for no-rewind after every dump, high density):

1. shutdown to single-user mode (hint: this has already been covered);

2. /sbin/bcheckrc (essay question: why?);

3. dump -0u -f /dev/nrmt0h -b BLOCKSIZE FILESYSTEM, where BLOCKSIZE is the
tape block size in Kbyte (40 is a nice number for DATs) and FILESYSTEM is,
e.g., / or /usr or /usr/users or /myfilesystem /go_eli or whatever.

    By the way, dump is virtually useless as far as data integrity goes,
but heck, this is unix.

    Seem like a lot of work? Remember what John F. Kennedy (who claimed,
on getting an honorary degree at Yale, that he then had the best of both
worlds: a Harvard education and a Yale degree) said: "Why do we wish to go
to the moon and do these other things? Why does Rice play Texas? We do
these things not because they are easy, but because they are hard."

    Good luck (and if you have a copy of the System Administrators guide,
take a look at the chapter on "Managing Backup and Restore Services"),

                  Joe Gurman

P.S. If your system (or department, or the University) has a DEC
Polycenter backup and restore license, you will have much better backup
options.... but space does not permit a full discussion. Class dismissed!

