>>>>> "Jonas" == Jonas Hvarfner <jonas@msiw30.msi.se> writes:
In article <3rimf2$rqt@news.kth.se> jonas@msiw30.msi.se (Jonas Hvarfner) writes:

Jonas> We are thinking of switching to AdvFS but from what I've read
Jonas> here it seems that AdvFS is a bit slow. We have a few very
Jonas> I/O-intensive (lot of read/write, lot of swap too) applications
Jonas> and it would be bad if they ran even slower than what they
Jonas> already do today. Thus I wonder if turning to AdvFS is such a
Jonas> good idea? We would like the special features of AdvFS but
Jonas> could of course do without them, maybe with the exeption of
Jonas> striping.

What you might try to do is move one partition from UFS to Advfs and
see how well your system runs then.  If you have lots of data, and you
need your system to come back as quickly as possible after a crash,
then Advfs is a win.  

Another performance tweak you could probably do is shove more memory
into the system, then up the buffers for disk IO.  This always helps.

Jonas> How is the performance of AdvFS compared to UFS? Is the
Jonas> difference significant? Is it worth using AdvFS if the only
Jonas> thing we really want is striping (over two disks in this case)?
Jonas> (BTW: we can't stripe without AdvFS, can we?)

The problem with Advfs for striping is that it only stripes files, not
filesets.  So if you need to have all you files striped, you're going
to need to get a RAID controller of some sort, or you can take a look
at the LSM product, which is a software based RAID system.  I have no
experience with it.

