In article <802994748snz@osil.demon.co.uk>, Dave Herdman <dherdman@osil.demon.co.uk> writes:

>I am evaluating a 2100 configuration that will require over 16 disks
>I believe this means having disks external to the 2100 cabinet.
>As I understand it the SWXCR controller will drop to 5MB/s transfer
>rate if it detects a longer cable path than 3 meters (?).
>
>Is this true ( I know that the internal setup is close to the limit
>for fast SCSI cable length) ?.

If the SWXCR negociates for Fast transfers on the backend busses,
then it would have to fall back to the slow speed if the cable
length exceeds that tolerated for Fast SCSI-2.  I don't know if
the SWXCR does the negociation, but I may be able to find out
if you're not sure.

>If so what are the options to have
>external storage (approx 40 RZ28s ) and maintain the higher transfer
>speed?

For configuring lots of drives, I'd get one or two PCI SCSI controllers
that support Fast/Wide/Differential and then suitable number of RAID
Array 410 subsystems (HSZ40s).  Differential support will give you
the distance you'll need to have lots of subsystems.

Using the SW300 configuration you can have up to 24 disks in a single
or dual controller configuration.  With the SW500 configuration you
can easily have 36-42 disks depending whether you have a dual or
single controller configuration and redundant or single power
supplies in the drive shelves.  The SW800 will support 3 controller
shelves with six drive shelves each.  If you're willing to have
less than six drives per shelf or split the shelves into two busses,
you can probably have more controller shelves.  The three controller
shelf configuration would allow 108 to 126 drives.

