In article <3r4u9r$psu@felix.seas.gwu.edu> ryentis@seas.gwu.edu (Richard D. Yentis) writes:

Richard> I am trying to use wu-ftpd on an a DEC Alpha running OSF/1 ver. 2.
Richard> When an anonymous user logs in the program does a chroot() to ~ftp.
Richard> This works, but all of the syslog()s after that do not produce entries
Richard> in the syslog file, although the syslog() function does return a 0
Richard> indicating that it was able to log the information.

syslog(3) does its stuff by sending messages to /dev/log, which of
course is not available after a chroot(2) call.  The OSF/1 sources I
have access to don't seem to have an option to force a connect(2) of the
syslog socket before chroot.  However, you can probably just pull the
relevant syslog.c files from your favorite 4.4BSD-Lite distribution and
use them instead.
