In Article <3r7n1k$1r2@expert.cc.purdue.edu>
snafu@expert.cc.purdue.edu (The Artist) writes:

>what's the difference between the following
>commands (besides the letter)
>
>mount /dev/rrz9c /mnt
>and 
>mount /dev/rz9c /mnt
>
>the only difference i immediately found is that
>"mount /dev/rrz9c /mnt" gives me a ":block device required"
>error, and "mount /dev/rz9c /mnt" mounts my drive
>with no apparent problems.  so, i put it to you...
>what does /dev/rrz9c imply that /dev/rz9c doesn't.
>they both point to the same device...don't they?

The /dev/rrz* disks are so called "raw" disk devices while /dev/rz* disks are
the actual, block addressable devices. For all file system access purposes you
use the /dev/rz* device. the raw device is used for formatting, labeling,
partitioning,  and such operations that access the disk at a low level. This in
not OSF/1 specific. It's common to most (all?) UNIX implementations.

The simple answer is that you don't mount "raw" devices as that interface
lacks the proper format to be mounted.

