fxars@camelot.acf-lab.alaska.edu wrote:
: We're running OSF/1 or Digital Unix with lots of users (6000
: accounts).  We're using the AdvFS, and C2 security (a decision we made
: after much debate).  It's an academic system, and our users are
: interactive.

: The hardware is a 7620.

: What I'd like to know is if there is anyone out there in our shoes.
: I'd like to compare notes, because we're running into a lot of
: problems (for example we're having problems with account creation;
: when we create accounts, we start to use up our root domain; when we
: reboot root domain goes back the way it was).

: Email, or post; I can go either way.
:   === Al


	We have a 7610 running OSF/1 3.0, C2 security and ADVfs for our
	home directories and research areas. We currently have almost
	4000 active accounts (after clearing off our spring semester 
	class accounts, we average approx. 40 new accounts/day). So it
	sounds like we are in pretty much the same category.

	As I read your description, it is the same problem that I had a
	couple of months ago. What C2 security does is in addition to adding
	information into /etc/passwd it also creates entries in the protected
	password database (aka the trusted computing base or tcb). Each entry
	is a file about 1-2kb in length located in /tcb/files/auth/x/y (where
	y is the login name and x is the first letter or the login name,
	i.e. /tcb/files/auth/r/ron would be my protected password entry.) What
	we did is to copy all of the tcb entries over to another 65mb
	partition and mounted it on /tcb. We also left a small number of tcb
	entries on the root partition /tcb directory (that gets hidden when
	the other partition is mounted) that contains root and our system
	development accounts. This was so we could still run the system
	multi-user if we lost the /tcb partition.

	We do not run ADVfs on the root partition because I am not
	that comfortable with it (we have had some severe problems
	with ADVfs on our news server, so bad that we had to send the
	disk drive with our ADVfs partitions to DEC engineering).

	This approach has worked out pretty good for us.

	let me know if you want further info...


