>The 9 GB do work, with some gotchas - mostly that you need more power
>and better cooling or they will overheat or blow power supply fuses.
>More so, they definitely do not work with the SWXCR or PCI version of
>the RAID controller.

The Seagate Elite 9GB drives (ST410800N) definitely *do* work with the
SWXCR -- I have a bunch hooked up to a 3-port SWXCR on a 2100.  The
drives do have to have firmware level 14 (or is it 17?) or above.
Mine are all in external cases, but one guy claims he has them working
on StorageWorks shelves.

The are a bunch of restrictions imposed by the SWXCR that make things
a bit annoying to configure large systems, like having a maximum
logical drive size of 32 GB, but these can be worked around.

Of course, it is pretty dumb to be trying to shovel about 100 MB/s max
down a 30 MB/s set of SCSI channels and then into a 16 MB/s EISA pipe.
Heck, the disks have more sustained *write* bandwidth than I can throw
at them.  I only went with the configuration because it was the
cheapest way to get a lot of on-line storage.

--David Gadbois




