organization: Rutgers University LCSR
newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

snafu@expert.cc.purdue.edu (The Artist) writes:

>what's the difference between the following
>commands (besides the letter)

>mount /dev/rrz9c /mnt

>and 

>mount /dev/rz9c /mnt

The main difference is that the second one works and the first one
doesn't.  "raw" devices (with the extra r) bypass some of the kernel's
buffering.  That may result in more efficiency for tape drives.  It's
also OK for fsck, since fsck hacks on the raw partition rather that
mounting the file system.  But the file system code is deeply
interwined with the disk buffer buffer management code, so you can
only use the non-raw ("block") version of the device for a mount.  I
don't claim to have used every version of Unix, but the only one I've
seen where this distinction does not exist is Linux.



