Hello Tony,

This 'rumor' is a favorite of the press, particularly the one that 
prints on yellow paper. Bill mentioned that we currently have no work 
ongoing to produce a 'major' release, but that we continue to support 
the code. I don't know if the same could be said for SVR4 anymore.

I manage OSF in the Pacific region and, at least as far as this region 
is concerned, OSF/1 is quite popular as a mature, standards-compliant, 
highly-performant, microkernel-based operating system. With all the 
turmoil and uncertainty in the unix source market since Novell purchased 
USL, we have enjoyed a resurgence of OSF/1 source sales. And because of 
the collaborative nature of the OSF/1 development, we can offer it at a 
very inexpensive license and binary price. Furthermore, the years of 
hard work put in by DEC, Hitachi, IBM and HP together with OSF have 
raised the quality of OSF/1 to among the top in the industry. Given that 
the first release in '90 was close to academic code, this is no small 
feat.

Also, watch the press for a major announcement in the next few weeks of 
a consortium of vendors, led by an Asian-based vendor, announcing OSF/1-
based systems on intel. 

Also, remember that the OSF Reseach Institute, which does advanced work 
on microkernel technology, uses the OSF/1 server as the primary base of 
it's research. The RI is funded over US$10+M per year to advance the 
area of microkernel-based operating system. Companies like IBM, Hitachi, 
HP, DEC, Apple, Novell, ATT GIS, Taligent, ETRI, etc.. participate in 
the RI Operating System Advisory Committee.

OSF will continue to provide OSF/1 to prospective licensees and while 
demand continues, we will continue to provide support.

The best kept secret in the industry.....

Regards,

