In article <3r7mpm$us@dub-news-svc-6.compuserve.com>, <100304.336@compuserve.com> writes:

>Hi,
>
>does somebody know how to calculate the minor numbers for devices
>which are connected to a HSZ40 storage works controller? I'm using
>KZTSA-AA as a SCSI adapter. I'm a bit confused with all the different 
>LUNs ports and SCSI busses!

If you have V3.2A (or later) installed use the script /dev/MAKE_RAID_LUNS
and don't worry about it.  For earlier versions, find one to send you
the script since it probably doesn't depend on anything particular
to V3.2.

For the logical unit 0 devices, just use the usual name:

	rz#; # = (bus * 8) + target-id

For non-LUN 0 devices you want to find a way to include the logical
unit in the name without creating names that will conflict with
other device names.  A common, but slightly broken convention is
to use the base drive number and then use a number for the logical
unit.  For example, the logical units for rz8 become rz80, rz81, etc.
Unfortunately, you could have enough SCSI busses on a system to have
an rz80, rz81, etc.

The naming convention used by the MAKE_RAID_LUNS script is:

	rz[b-h]#[a-h]

where, the first letter after the rz is the logical unit:

	b = LUN 1
	c = LUN 2
	etc.

You don't need to use A for LUN 0, because that is just the
base rz# name.

If you need to calculate the minor device numbers:

(bus * 16384) + (target * 1024) + (LUN * 64) + partition

(This is documented in the SCSI(7) manual page.)

