In article <D9rq6J.EIp@world.std.com>, gnewman@world.std.com (Gary Newman) writes:

>I am running OSF/1 v3.2 on a 233 Mhz AlphaStation 400.
>Recently, I've been getting occasional messages such
>as the following in the console log window:
>
>    UBC write error(4) on device (0, 0) at block 21216 size 8192
>
>Does anyone know the meaning of this message?  Should I
>be worried?

Digital UNIX V3.2 reports NFS write errors.  The error(#) is
probably the errno value associated with the error.  4 is
probably an interrupted system call.  The block and size
may be valid, but I've seen some examples where I had to
wonder about the block number.

>I ran the 'verify media' scu command on
>"bus 0, target 0, lun 0", and things seemed fine.  Is there a 
>better disk diagnostic?

Scu's "verify media" is fine.  This error probably wasn't caused
by a disk error.  And if it was it was an error on the NFS for
whatever file system had the error.  Unfortunately, that isn't
in the error listing.  A future version should fix the message
so that it will only be used when appropriate.

>Thanks for any help you can provide.
>
>    Bill
>
>P.S.	Does anyone know how to map these block numbers to
>	cylinder, head, sector numbers?  I'd like to see
>	if there is any pattern to the blocks reported.
>	Thanks.  It's the standard 1GB SCSI drive delivered
>	with the system.

This is getting really, really hard these days.  I think
all Alpha systems prefer the RZ26L as their standard 1 GB
disk.  The RZ26L is a zoned based recording disk and so
has different geometries depending on while cylinder you're
in.  I think it has four bands.  If you can figure out the
detailed geometry and whether any sectors are used for
replacement on a per-track basis, then a good first guess
for the physical location of an LBN should be relatively
easy.  Of course the LBN should have been revectored at
some point and you'd need need to look at the disk internals
to figure out where it really is.

