> look at the disk.
> 
> When booting the system recognises the drive (device rz4), but every
> time I try to mount the drive I get an "IO Error". I am assuming I
> am using the correct mount command:
>         "mount -d -r /dev/rz4c /cdrom" (or something similar).
> 
> Any clues as to what I am doing wrong would be helpful.

    Grant -

    Is the device included in the kernel? (One way to check is to grep for
'rz4' in /sys/conf/YOUR_SYSTEM_NAME, where your system's name is all
uppercase. If not, you need to follow the screed in the System Admin's
guide to adding a disk device (basically, rebuild the kernel with doconfig
and do a cd /dev ; ./MAKEDEV  rz4).

    Even if the device is included in your kernel, you need to have the
mount point /cdrom. For some reason, DEC refers to this mount point in
their docs, but doesn't include it in the out-of-the-box OS (at least,
through 3.0). Just mkdir /cdrom. (You can, of course, use anything as a
mountpoint: /mnt, &c.)

    Hope this helps,

                  Joe Gurman

