In article <3r4ad8$14f@acara.snsnet.net>, aaron@garply.com writes:
>
>Does anyone have a disktab entry for a Connor CFP1080S, a 1.08 GB
>3-1/2" drive?  We just got a drive "given" to us and it came with
>no documentation, so we don't even know the geometry (or the 
>manufacturer's phone number!!!)  We'd like to mount it as a 
>secondary swap disk, but can't do a disklabel...

The disklabel command should be able to get the geometry from the
driver, if the drive is well behaved.  If disklabel can't get
the geometry, then you have to work harder.  SCSI drives store
the geometry in one of the mode pages.  You can use the scu(8)
command to show the contents of these pages (show page).  I
think you need the "rigid" and "direct" pages.

From this you can get a clue about the geometry and figure
one out.  If all you're going to use the drive is page/swap
space then the geometry isn't important.  If you want to use
the whole drive for page/swap, don't bother labeling and just
use the C partition.

If you're having some other problem with disklabel, please
transcribe the error message.

