> We recently got a DEC Alpha and I have a couple of problems with the
> freeware on it.  Firstly, ghostview (or rather, ghostscript),
> doesn't quite work.  The gs binary supplied by them doesn't execute
> at all.  When I type "gs", it prints:

> /usr/freeware/binmx/gs: b^A^HL$B*8^G^K^A: not found
> /usr/freeware/binmx/gs: syntax error at line 7: '(' unexpected

I've found that alot of the stuff on the Freeware disc doesn't work
(e.g. "trace" or "truss", whatever they're calling it.)

> So I got gs2.61 from somewhere and installed it.  It almost works,
> but not quite.  On most files, the fonts are quite ugly.  I
> installed the fonts that came with gs2.61 in
> /usr/local/lib/ghostscript, but it looks like I am still missing
> something.  Upon zooming, everything looks okay, but then, I don't
> want to look at zoomed text.


I built 2.61 and was able to get it working fine, except when used
within ghostview.  Generic PS programs that display just fine in gs
didn't work when viewed with ghostview (which calls gs.)  It would
complain about a "malformed color property" or somesuch.
Correspondence with the bugs address given in the package revealed
that there was a bug in the X11 driver in gs 2.61 and that this was
fixed in 2.62.  2.62 however had problems with 64-bit archs.  He then
told me to get 3.33 which fixed both problems.  I didn't even know
there *was* a 3.33 since it didn't show up on any archie database I
had visited, or I would have tried that first.  He helped me find it
at ftp.cs.wisc.edu:/ghost/aladdin/ghostscript-3.33*.  I also grabbed
the most recent font package (which may solve your problem) from the
same place.

After doing all this, a colleague of mine pointed out to me that you
can view PostScript directly on the Alpha's X11 server using the
following command:

	% dxvdoc -f ps FILENAME



