Oh yeah, are you listening?

Meanwhile, I got the A2100 to load the kernel by installing a
proxy ARP daemon on the gateway.  There are PD versions for
SunOS-4.1* in

ftp://aun.uninett.no/pub/unix/proxyarpd-1.7.shar.Z

and for Ultrix in

ftp://ftp.inria.net/networking/misc/ulx-proxyarp.shar.gz.

But of course that alone would be far to easy... after booting,
the helpful message

Cannot determine RIS home directory on

is issued and the shell prompt appears.

Trying to mount the RIS environment from the server by hand
won't work (even after adding the client and the server
in /etc/hosts - which contains only the loopback address):

# mount -t nfs 131.188.33.21:/var/adm/ris/ris0.alpha/kit /kit
Can't get net id for host

And running ifconfig (from the memory file system) shows that
the network interface isn't configured:

# ifconfig tu0
tu0: flags=c63<UP,BROADCAST,NOTRAILERS,RUNNING,MULTICAST,SIMPLEX>
     inet 0.0.0.0 netmask 0  ipmtu 1500

Trying to configure it e.g. by

# ifconfig tu0 inet 131.188.43.22 netmask 0xffffff00 broadcast 255.255.255.0

won't work (without *any* error message this time, it just does
nothing).  So the ifconfig program from the installation kernel
seems to be a complete noop.

I thought that installing SunOS was a painful process, but this
really gets annoying:
- Having to install a new firmware release (which requires
  physical access to the hardware - how the heck are you supposed
  to update dozens or hundreds of machines??) for each new version
  of the OS.
- This wonderful new firmware contains unnecessary junk like a
  menu-based configuration utility and a command line editor (com-
  pare this with having to enter partition tables by using ed - I
  really have nothing to say against ed, but IMHO it's ridiculous
  to put menu systems in the firmware and on the other hand fall
  back to ed for the installation after booting a complete OS)
  but not even real TFTP.
- Having to install and to configure lots of auxiliary software
  (for which there is no pointer in the installation guide) on
  machines that have nothing to do with the installation just 
  because DEC doesn't see it fit to support their own FDDI con-
  trollers.
- And then being confronted with a system that loads over 9MB of
  software and still isn't able to mount something via NFS from
  another subnet.

I really thought that OSF would be a bit more suitable for a
modern networking installation - but if things go on like this,
I can only hope that Linux/Alpha is released soon...

Stefan
