In article <3qvps0$fbk@pt9201.ped.pto.ford.com> millerb@pt9542.ped.pto.ford.com (Bryan Miller) writes:

>   I am running a ksh script in the background with
>   nohup.  I am getting a plethora of "Where are you?"
>   queries.  What is responsible for sending these messages
>   and why does it care where I am?

that script is running your .profile or .kshrc or whatever.  there may
be a way to turn that off for ksh; for csh/tcsh, one runs the script
with #!/bin/csh -f.

anyway, the problem is that you have commands in there that affect
your terminal.  usually, these are things like "stty", "tset", and
"biff".  i even once read of a case with a "talk".  you want to clear
out most everything that isn't a shell built-in command (setting
variables and the like).  generally, one puts that in the .login.

