In article <3qhse4$r6i@taurus.fccc.edu>, mathew@aquila.fccc.edu (George Mathew) writes:

>     Has anyone successfully installed a 9 Gb drive on an
>Alpha/OSF1 machine?

I haven't, but the shouldn't affect the answers...

>What I am interested in knowing is specifically:
>
>      1. Will the disklabel command work on the drive
>         without any problems?

Any problem?  Only one that I know of.  If you really care
that the disklabel has the correct rotational speed of the
disk, you'll have to edit the label afterward.  Disklabel
uses 3600 rpm for everything, even all the drives that are
in /etc/disktab.  I'm told this will be fixed in a future
release.

>      2. Is there any problem in using partitions more than
>         2 Gb on the drive?

Shouldn't be.

>      3. Is there any system utility (like dump or restore)
>         or application that will fail to work on partitions
>         more than 2 Gb?

Depends on the version you run.  I did some large device testing
way back in V1.2 and one of ncheck or icheck was still using 32
bit offsets.  That was fixed though.

>
>      4. Any other known problems?
>
>  (I said 2 Gb in the above questions because DEC doesn't
>   support drives more than 2 Gb and I read an article
>   sometimes back that somebody had a problem with
>   partition more than 2 Gb. I don't whether it is fixed.)

Digital UNIX (formerly DEC OSF/1) has supported larger than 2 GB
partitions since the first Alpha release of V1.2.  I think the
first file system size limit was 32 GB (since raised to 128 GB).
ULTRIX has a 2 GB limit, but that's not surprising for a system
stuck with 32 bit longs.

There's an old dump bug fixed around V2.0 that many people
thought was size related, but I'm not sure it was.

